# Understand Bubble Mart (4): What will be the profit in 3 years? Should I vote?

**Series Finale**

In the first three articles, we dismantled Bubble Mart’s growth engine, global benchmarks and moats. This article answers the last question: **How ​​much money can this company make in the next 3 to 5 years? Is it worth investing at the current price? **

Don’t beat around the bush, just count.

---

## 1. First set the known conditions

Before making predictions, known "anchors" must be nailed down.

| Indicators | Data |
|------|------|
| Net profit in 2025 | 12.8 billion (adjusted 13.08 billion) |
| Net profit margin in 2025 | 35.2% |
| Growth rate in the first quarter of 2026 | 75-80% |
| Management’s full-year guidance for 2026 | Revenue growth will not be less than 20% |

This set of data contains an extremely eye-catching figure: **35.2% net interest rate**.

In the consumer goods industry, there are only a handful of companies in the world that have maintained a net profit margin of 35% for a long time. For comparison: Lego’s net profit margin is about 20%, Bandai’s is just over 10%, Nike’s 12%, and Hermès’s net profit margin is about 30%—and Hermès is recognized as the profit ceiling for consumer goods.

Bubble Mart’s profit margin in 2025 will be higher than Hermès.

This is not a compliment, this is a reminder of the risks. A net profit margin of 35% either indicates that the company is in an extremely favorable window period (insufficient competition, category explosion, overseas is in the "harvest" rather than "investment" stage), or that it is an anomaly in the history of human consumer goods.

I prefer the former.

---

## 2nd and 3rd year forecast: to 2028

There are only two core variables: **Revenue annual compound growth rate** and **Net interest rate center**.

| Scenario | Revenue compound growth rate | Net profit margin assumption | 2028 revenue | 2028 net profit |
|------|------------|-----------|-----------|-------------|
| Optimistic | 30% | 30% | ~81.5 billion | ~24.5 billion |
| Neutral | 20% | 25% | ~64 billion | ~16 billion |
| Pessimistic | 10% | 20% | ~49.5 billion | ~9.9 billion |

**Explanation of several key assumptions:**

It is a high probability event that the net interest rate will drop from 35%. There are three reasons: Overseas expansion has moved from "light asset testing" to a "heavy investment in channel expansion" stage; competitors are competing for the market, and channel costs will rise; categories are spreading from blind boxes to plush and building blocks, and each new category has its own cost structure.

A revenue compound growth rate of 30% means maintaining a high expansion rate for three consecutive years. But it is still difficult to maintain 30% for three years - the base is getting larger and larger, and new growth curves must continue to be relayed.

The meaning of the pessimistic scenario: profits fall instead of rising. It doesn't sound catastrophic, but for a company priced for "high growth" by the market, falling profits means the stock price may be cut in half.

